docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-23-canlin-core-api-design.md §6 records the payload of an OP completion as 'canlin_module_result {value | code, message}'. Since canlin#88 the struct begins with a uint32_t tag (CANLIN_MODULE_RESULT_TAG), and the payload is the producer's blob whose first bytes are that struct. The spec should say so. Found by the 2026-11-14 work on k88.

2026-11-14 23:41 Review 2026-11-14: fixed and reviewed. The core API spec §6 records the operation result as 'canlin_module_result {tag, value | code, message}' and a new bullet says what the tag is for: the payload rides in the producer's blob, the struct begins with CANLIN_MODULE_RESULT_TAG ('MRES'), the tag is checked before a single field of the body is read, a payload of another shape arrives as ok=false, code=unavailable and its words are never followed as pointers, and the block may carry the producer's own data behind the result because the pointers in the result must stay valid until the delivery. The worker checked it against include/canlin/module.h:72-84, src/module/module.c:451-495, src/app/assistant.c:364-395 and src/llm/llm.c:801,882. Committed as 9a20954, together with k100 - the same file.
